#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180358} :activity/id #uuid "6dfc0cb1-b79c-5ad6-b36f-4f27bb5a1468" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050210 :topic/id #uuid "369e4b42-eb06-50fe-866b-3c2859ec00eb" :topic/math-academy-id 3753} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180358 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180737 :tutorial/content "We can use trend lines to predict values for data that follows a linear trend.\n\nConsider the scatter plot below that gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y.$ Let's predict the value of $y$ when $x = 5.$\n\n![](images/8a/8ae392764c110d85f913353ddd932fad9d9c7fedde6275c2705cc7f3e92d3844.png)\n\nFirst, we sketch the trend line. If we don't know the trend line's equation, then we simply approximate it.\n\n![](images/2e/2e001c3f1e9dbdc2574913116a0fb73ac70396e97777c56b9d36b683d10d9814.png)\n\nTo make our prediction, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we start at $x = 5$ on the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/fb/fb4f4ac52e4e2190b54cf9cf272e720729635c48387e6ac439ffcaf1282a2d7d.png)\n\n- Then, we move up until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](images/46/46a2e566d3810362756e6061e3120b7e7140a8a71591a6a1b90fd059b0505ae0.png)\n\n- Finally, we move left until we intersect the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/8d/8db3717a601f2e5b22c507385a04c60531d2ed057adc5372c5dddba174cf703f.png)\n\nTherefore, when $x = 5,$ we obtain $y \\approx 10.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "45f3d201-7eff-5359-a7a1-84d34c8e0e11" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5947 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3c1fdb21-a077-5b08-bb0d-cad183c76182" :step/math-academy-id 194360 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180359}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180359 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179653 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179615 :question/id #uuid "1900f9ee-5295-5e56-b9d5-2dda8649b212" :question/math-academy-id "e-9684"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "892f45fd-1e75-5500-90c3-0218fab5f1e1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050516}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549958}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Lines to Make Predictions"} :step/id #uuid "e0ae380d-48eb-5e47-a77c-d9f4180601d4" :step/math-academy-id 194361 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180360}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180360 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180738 :tutorial/content "If we know the equation of a trend line, we can make more accurate predictions about a given data set. Let's consider an example.\n\nThe pediatric unit of a hospital wants to know the relationship between the age $x$ (in years) and weight $y$ (in kilograms) of their patients. They collected data over several weeks and plotted the diagram given below. \n\n![](images/b6/b63e7db30ad2533058b9e2191f699043784a42b2b3f73525783f720cf84e0fc5.png)\n\nThe hospital finds that the trend line equation is $y = 2x+8.$\n\nNow, suppose the hospital wants to estimate the weight of a $3$-year-old child.  To do this, we substitute $x=3$ into the equation of the trend line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x+8 \\\\  & =2(3)+8 \\\\  & =6+8 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, our model predicts that a typical $3$-year-old patient weighs approximately $14\\,\\textrm{kg}.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- There is a point on our trend line where $x=3.$ Therefore, the result $y=14$ should be reliable since we can see from the graph that the data follows a linear trend for values of $x$ close to $3.$\n\n- Making predictions inside the range of values from which the data was collected is known as **interpolation**." :tutorial/id #uuid "6f9baaae-e720-5cb3-a975-cc7467e70fca" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5948 :tutorial/title "Make Predictions Using the Trend Line Equation"} :step/id #uuid "e74c6fb4-ea08-5445-b829-46c0e53a1faf" :step/math-academy-id 194364 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180361}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180361 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180751 :tutorial/content "![](images/9c/9cac9d494a95fab39dc7fd71f2d0daf9d474157d453dac61b25ab71da4cc580f.png)\n\nNow suppose that the hospital wants to estimate the weight of a $7$-year-old child. They have no data for this age. However, if they assume that the trend continues, they can find the corresponding weight by substituting $x=7$ into the trend line equation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x+8 \\\\  & =2(7)+8 \\\\  & =14+8 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, our model predicts that a typical $7$-year-old child weighs approximately $22 \\, \\textrm{kg}.$\n\n**Warning:** The point where $x=7$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, the result $y=22$ *may be unreliable* since we don't know whether values of $x$ close to $7$ follow the same linear trend. The diagram below shows some trends the data *might* follow beyond our initial data range.\n\n![](images/9e/9ea0d5bde6ac6854ea9992cac38f3bf06acb54b0065fff8174d75c89992203b0.png)\n\nMaking predictions outside the range of values from which the data was collected is known as **extrapolation**.\n\nWe must be very cautious when extrapolating in real-world situations." :tutorial/id #uuid "ece1fab5-157e-5d98-8247-d12f922daf94" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6014 :tutorial/title "Extrapolation"} :step/id #uuid "350c6591-826e-553f-b53b-8091df2b9360" :step/math-academy-id 194365 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180362}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180362 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179654 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179617 :question/id #uuid "3a864a65-e753-5bd5-b4fd-b3e95fc5a2de" :question/math-academy-id "e-9692"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28adc95e-e6e5-580c-9eed-55a3d12d5232" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049200}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550016}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Line Equations to Make a Prediction"} :step/id #uuid "e3ec61b0-1600-591b-8322-939ae6586700" :step/math-academy-id 194366 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180363}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180363 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179655 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179616 :question/id #uuid "2bc871c4-b557-5cff-8ab2-55d9cb6e24fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-9690"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "877509f5-a5de-55d2-ab5d-6c37fc5b0650" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049394}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550011}] :knowledge-point/title "Making a Prediction by Estimating a Trend Line"} :step/id #uuid "528377e3-e010-5ca3-979e-8aa3d425121f" :step/math-academy-id 194369}] :activity/title "Making Predictions Using Trend Lines" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179896}]}
